---
import ComparisonTable from "@components/article/ComparisonTable.astro";
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

// Article metadata
const title = "Best Banking for Digital Nomads (2025)";
const description =
  "Traditional banks weren't built for people like us. I was losing hundreds monthly to exchange rates and transfer fees. Here's how to stop the bleeding.";
const authorSlug = "daniel-brooks";
const pubDate = new Date("2025-11-23");
const readingTime = 4;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1563986768494-4dee2763ff3f-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "Mobile banking app on smartphone with credit cards";

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  { text: "Why This Matters", slug: "introduction", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Quick Comparison", slug: "quick-comparison", depth: 2 },
  { text: "What Actually Matters", slug: "what-to-look-for", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Accounts", slug: "reviews", depth: 2 },
  { text: "My Stack", slug: "strategies", depth: 2 },
  { text: "FAQ", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Bottom Line", slug: "conclusion", depth: 2 },
];

// Comparison table
const comparisonColumns = [
  { key: "bank", label: "Account", width: "100px" },
  { key: "currencies", label: "Currencies" },
  { key: "fees", label: "Monthly Fee" },
  { key: "atm", label: "Free ATM" },
  { key: "bestFor", label: "Best For" },
];

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    bank: "<strong>Wise</strong>",
    currencies: "40+",
    fees: "$0",
    atm: "$100-200/mo",
    bestFor: "Currency exchange",
  },
  {
    bank: "<strong>Revolut</strong>",
    currencies: "30+",
    fees: "$0-13",
    atm: "$200-400/mo",
    bestFor: "All-in-one",
  },
  {
    bank: "<strong>Mercury</strong>",
    currencies: "USD only",
    fees: "$0",
    atm: "$0",
    bestFor: "US business",
  },
  {
    bank: "<strong>Payoneer</strong>",
    currencies: "4",
    fees: "$0-30",
    atm: "$3.15/txn",
    bestFor: "Freelancer payments",
  },
  {
    bank: "<strong>Charles Schwab</strong>",
    currencies: "USD",
    fees: "$0",
    atm: "Unlimited",
    bestFor: "US citizens abroad",
  },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "No fixed address—can I open these?",
    answer:
      "<p>Wise and Revolut just need ID verification. Traditional banks want addresses. Use a family member's or a virtual mailbox.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Will my home bank close my account?",
    answer:
      "<p>Maybe. US banks generally don't care. UK and EU vary. Keep it active with occasional transactions.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I receive international payments?",
    answer:
      "<p>Wise and Payoneer give you local bank details. Clients pay locally, you receive in your currency. No wire fees.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Emergency cash access?",
    answer:
      "<p>Multiple cards from different providers. Charles Schwab for unlimited ATM. Keep some USD/EUR cash.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need a business account?",
    answer:
      "<p>Not legally required. But it separates finances for taxes, looks professional, and often has higher limits.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Tax Tips for Remote Workers",
    excerpt: "Navigate taxes as a remote worker or digital nomad.",
    href: "/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1554224155-6726b3ff858f-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Tax documents and calculator",
  },
  {
    title: "Crypto-Friendly Banks for Remote Workers",
    excerpt: "Traditional banks that work well with cryptocurrency.",
    href: "/blog/crypto-friendly-banks",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1518546305927-5a555bb7020d-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Cryptocurrency concept",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Travel Insurance for Remote Workers",
    excerpt: "Coverage designed for long-term travelers.",
    href: "/blog/best-travel-insurance-remote-workers",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1436491865332-7a61a109cc05-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Travel planning",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="tools/finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Why This Matters</h2>
  <p>
    So there I was in Chiang Mai, checking my bank statement at a cafe. Three ATM withdrawals that week.
    $5 fee each. Currency conversion on every card swipe—another 3%. A wire transfer from a US client?
    $45 gone. I did the math. $847 in bank fees over four months.
  </p>
  <p>
    That was the expensive lesson that made me figure this out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Traditional banks weren't built for us. Earn in dollars, spend in baht, get paid in pounds—they
    punish you for it. But fintech changed everything. Multi-currency accounts, real exchange rates,
    local bank details in countries you've never lived in. The right setup? I cut those fees to under
    $50 a month.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Comparison -->
  <h2 id="quick-comparison">Quick Comparison</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={comparisonColumns} rows={comparisonRows}/>

  <p>
    <strong>My picks:</strong> Wise for exchange rates. Revolut for everything in one app. Charles
    Schwab if you're American and want free ATMs everywhere.
  </p>

  <!-- What to Look For -->
  <h2 id="what-to-look-for">What Actually Matters</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Exchange Rates:</strong> The mid-market rate is the real rate. Banks mark up 2-4%.
    Wise and Revolut? Mid-market plus a tiny fee (0.3-0.6%). That difference adds up fast.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Foreign Transaction Fees:</strong> Most cards charge 1-3% on "foreign" purchases. When
    every purchase is foreign, that's brutal. Get a 0% fee card.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>ATM Access:</strong> Some accounts give $200-400/month free. Others charge per withdrawal.
    Charles Schwab? Unlimited refunds worldwide. That's the one.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Multi-Currency:</strong> Hold balances in USD, EUR, GBP without converting back and forth.
    Saves on repeated conversions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>International Transfers:</strong> Banks charge $25-50 to send money internationally.
    Plus terrible rates. Wise charges a fraction.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Card Security:</strong> Virtual cards, instant freezing, transaction alerts. Fintech
    apps have this. Traditional banks usually don't.
  </p>

  <!-- Top Accounts Reviewed -->
  <h2 id="reviews">The Accounts</h2>

  <h3 id="wise">Wise</h3>
  <p>
    This is what I use for currency stuff. 40+ currencies. Local bank details in 10+ countries so
    clients can pay me without wire fees.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mid-market exchange rate plus 0.3-0.6%. No monthly fee. $100-200 free ATM per month.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Real exchange rates. Local receiving accounts. Just works.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> Not a real bank. No overdraft. Limited interest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/wise" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-jet-stream hover:text-sky-light">
      Open Wise Account &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3 id="revolut">Revolut</h3>
  <p>
    Banking, investing, crypto—all in one app. Free tier is solid. Premium adds travel insurance
    and higher ATM limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    30+ currencies. Mid-market rates on weekdays (small markup on weekends). Free tier is $0,
    Premium is $13/month.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Everything in one place. Good app.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> Support can be slow. Weekend exchange rates aren't great.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/revolut" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-jet-stream hover:text-sky-light">
      Open Revolut Account &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3 id="mercury">Mercury</h3>
  <p>
    Modern business banking. US only, but if you're American and <a href="/guides/freelancer-tax-deductions-guide">freelancing</a>,
    this is it.
  </p>
  <p>
    No monthly fee. Virtual cards. Accounting integrations. Clean interface.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Finally, business banking that doesn't feel like 2003.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> US only. USD only.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/mercury" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-jet-stream hover:text-sky-light">
      Open Mercury Account &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3 id="payoneer">Payoneer</h3>
  <p>
    Built for <a href="/tools/accept-international-payments-freelancer">receiving payments</a> from Upwork, Fiverr,
    Amazon, clients. You get local bank details
    so people can pay you locally.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Easy to receive international payments.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> 2% withdrawal fee. Higher fees than Wise overall.
  </p>

  <h3 id="schwab">Charles Schwab</h3>
  <p>
    The unlimited ATM hack. Every ATM fee worldwide, refunded. No foreign transaction fees. $0
    monthly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Catch: You need to open a brokerage account too (can leave it empty).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Never worry about ATM fees again.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> US citizens only. USD only. No multi-currency.
  </p>

  <!-- Banking Strategies -->
  <h2 id="strategies">My Stack</h2>

  <p>Most nomads end up with 2-3 accounts:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Daily spending:</strong> Wise or Revolut card</li>
    <li><strong>ATM backup:</strong> Charles Schwab (US) or similar</li>
    <li><strong>Home bank:</strong> Keep it active for taxes, government stuff</li>
    <li><strong>Business:</strong> Mercury or Wise Business if <a href="/tools/best-invoicing-tools">freelancing</a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Getting Paid Internationally</h3>
  <p>Use <a href="/tools/wise-vs-paypal-vs-payoneer">Wise or Payoneer's</a> local bank details:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>US clients pay to your US account (no wire fees for them)</li>
    <li>EU clients pay to your EUR account</li>
    <li>Convert at mid-market when you need to</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Saving on Fees</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Always pay in local currency (decline "pay in your currency")</li>
    <li>Batch conversions—fewer, larger amounts</li>
    <li>Match card to currency when possible</li>
    <li>Never use airport exchanges. Ever.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Emergency Backup</h3>
  <p>Cards get lost. Stolen. Frozen for "suspicious activity" while you're abroad.</p>
  <p>Always have:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Two debit cards from different providers</li>
    <li>One credit card</li>
    <li>$200-500 cash in USD/EUR</li>
    <li>Virtual cards for online stuff</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/wise" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wise</a> for exchange rates.
    <a href="/go/revolut" rel="nofollow sponsored">Revolut</a> if you want everything in one app.
    Charles Schwab if you're American and hate ATM fees.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open accounts before you leave. Verification is easier with a stable address and phone number.
  </p>
  <p>
    Takes some setup. But the right accounts save hundreds and make international life way smoother.
  </p>
  <p>
    Also check: <a href="/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers">taxes for remote workers</a> and
    <a href="/blog/crypto-friendly-banks">crypto-friendly banks</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
